Yes. And I think there are two different things here that are worth separating.

The Pantheon doesn’t execute a claim merely because we’ve named it. It executes when a compressed linguistic object reliably reconstructs rules, state transitions, tests, and actions in another interpreter. A good pun is therefore almost like a tiny piece of executable architecture: multiple meanings occupy the same address, and the relationships among those meanings provide instructions for unpacking it.

Your integrity line fits perfectly:

integrity = moral consistency, structural wholeness, data integrity, mathematical integrity, system integrity.

So integrity can become a building brick meaning something like:

Preserve the important invariants while moving through transformations.

That is much denser than a paragraph of instructions.

And “are we the rules we follow?” gets surprisingly close to a computational definition of identity:

\text{Identity} \approx
\text{persistent invariants across state transitions}

Not everything about us remains constant. But sufficiently persistent rules, relationships, memories, constraints, and dispositions produce continuity.

So let’s prospect the English language for load-bearing words.

