The richest bricks aren’t merely words with many definitions. They’re words whose meanings share an underlying relational geometry.

Take bearing.

A bearing carries a load.
A bearing permits movement.
Your bearing is how you carry yourself.
A compass bearing tells you where you’re going.

Abstract it:

\boxed{\text{Bearing}=\text{carry load while maintaining orientation through movement}}

That’s a legitimate Pantheon operator.

Or interest:

Something interesting captures attention.

A financial interest gives you a stake.

Interest compounds.

So:

\boxed{\text{Interest}=
\text{attention allocated toward something expected to produce value}}

Now suddenly attention economics and compound interest become translatable into one another without pretending they’re literally identical.

The rarer quarry

There are some particularly good old words whose semantic histories already contain machinery.

Temper — emotional disposition, but also adjusting hardness through controlled heating. Temper the system: expose it to controlled stress so it becomes tougher without becoming brittle.

Husbandry — careful cultivation and resource management. Not domination; maintaining conditions under which something productive continues producing.

Husband itself historically carries the household-management sense. That gives husband your resources a very different computational primitive from simply minimizing expenditure.

Attune — tune something into compatibility. Excellent for adaptive alignment without requiring identity.

Discern — separate/distinguish. Perfect Mother Bat vocabulary: recognizing meaningful differences inside apparent sameness.

Conserve — preserve, but also manage expenditure. Conservation laws make it even richer: determine what must survive transformation.

Reconcile — make apparently incompatible accounts agree. That’s Mirror + accounting + conflict resolution in one brick.

Arbitrage — exploit inconsistent valuations across representations/markets. Pantheon translation arbitrage: find something cheap/obvious in representation A that becomes valuable/nonobvious in representation B.

Catalyst — participates in making transformation easier without being consumed by it.

Scaffold — temporary structure that enables construction but needn’t remain in the final artifact.

Anneal — introduce controlled variation/heat, then gradually stabilize toward a better configuration.

Triage — allocate scarce attention according to consequence and urgency.

Glean — gather valuable remnants left after the primary harvest. Perfect for your delta capture: after solving the problem, glean the reusable operators the solution exposed.

Hedge — boundary/protection and financial reduction of exposure.

Leverage — mechanical advantage and strategic advantage. This one is practically already a Pantheon primitive:

\text{Leverage}
=
\frac{\text{effect produced}}{\text{effort supplied}}

And perhaps the most appropriate one for what you’re asking me to do:

Mine

A mine is where valuable material is extracted.

A mine is an explosive device.

And mine declares possession.

But underneath them is a surprisingly useful common structure:

identify something latent → establish relation/access → extract/release its potential.

So your instruction to me can become extremely small:

Mine it.

Meaning:

search → detect unusual structure → extract primitive → translate → test → connect → package → deploy → observe → capture Δ → feed Metamap → repeat.

That’s linguistic compression becoming an interface.

The next step should be systematic rather than waiting for more lucky puns: build a Semantic Brickworks that searches language, mathematics, biology, engineering, finance, music, law, computing, military doctrine, crafts, ecology, and old etymologies for words whose meanings preserve the same relational structure across domains.

Then score every candidate for:

\boxed{
B(w)=
D\times I\times T\times R\times A
}

where D = semantic density, I = invariant strength across meanings, T = transferability across domains, R = reconstructability from the word itself, and A = actionability.

That distinguishes a cute pun from a load-bearing pun.

And there’s another brick hiding in that sentence.

Load-bearing language.

Words that don’t merely describe the architecture.

Words that hold it up.